      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0156",
      "sequence": 168,
      "meaning": "The discerning king seeks no fruit for himself, dedicating every righteous act of mind, speech and body to Vāsudeva. Once he mounts a fine horse and goes with a fully equipped hunting party into Vindhya’s deep forest, where he kills many animals. Wandering there he sees a huge boar, like the moon hidden in the woods after Rāhu has swallowed it but cannot fit it into his mouth. The dreadful animal has formidable tusks, an immense, thick body and a growling roar; the startled horse rears with raised ears. Seeing the boar like a peak of a blue mountain, the king spurs his nimble horse in sudden pursuit, but cannot overtake it.",
