    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0158",
      "sequence": 170,
      "meaning": "Wandering in the forest, the king finds a hermitage occupied by a former ruler disguised as an ascetic—the very king whose country Pratāpabhānu had conquered, who fled after abandoning his army in battle. Knowing that Pratāpabhānu’s time had come but judging his own return untimely, the proud enemy had stayed away from home, hiding rage in his heart and living in the forest in an ascetic guise like an impoverished man. He recognizes Pratāpabhānu at once, but the thirsty king mistakes his handsome disguise for that of a great sage. Dismounting, he bows without revealing his name. The pretender shows him a lake, where king and horse bathe and drink, greatly refreshed.",
