    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0159",
      "sequence": 171,
      "meaning": "With all fatigue gone, the contented king is led to the hermitage. Knowing the sun has set, the ascetic asks who he is and why a handsome young man marked as an emperor wanders alone in the dangerous forest. Pratāpabhānu conceals his identity, claiming to be the king’s minister who became lost while hunting, and calls it great fortune to have reached the sage’s feet. The pretender says darkness has fallen, the city is seventy yojanas away, and the night road through the deep forest is perilous, so the king should stay and depart at dawn. Tulsīdāsa observes that destiny itself brings the helper who leads a person where fate intends.",
