    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0160",
      "sequence": 172,
      "meaning": "Accepting the command, the king ties his horse beneath a tree, sits, praises the ascetic and blesses his own fortune. Addressing him as a father, he ventures to ask the sage’s name so that he may serve him as a son. The disguised enemy knows the king, though the king does not know him. As enemy, warrior and former king, the cunning man wants to achieve his purpose through deceit; smouldering like a covered fire from lost royal happiness, he inwardly rejoices as Pratāpabhānu speaks with guileless trust. Dipping gentle words in fraud, he says his name is Bhikhāri, a homeless pauper with no possessions.",
